WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
LIONEL APETOH: I would say many individuals&nbsp;
like they are one way or the other familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.460
with cancer and actually my mother&nbsp;
died of breast cancer. And my goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:16.260
is basically to try to endow T cells&nbsp;
with better anti-cancer properties.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:31.248
LA:&nbsp;T cells are a subset of white blood cells that&nbsp;
are able to contribute to fighting off infections.

00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:37.920
VOICE OVER: Lionel Apetoh is an immunologist that&nbsp;
has been studying T cells for more than 15 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:47.520
LA: I got interested in T cells like because during my&nbsp;
PhD we were wondering to find how chemotherapies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:55.560
could somehow, in precise situation, harness the&nbsp;
immune system. And I remember that was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.425 --> 00:01:06.000
key experiments I did at the time, was to compare&nbsp;
how wild type mice, with a full immune system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.360
and immune deficient mice, lacking T&nbsp;
cells, respond to local radiotherapy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:21.720
And actually we found that T cells were essential&nbsp;
for the efficacy of chemotherapy. If mice were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:28.620
devoid of those T cells they were not responding&nbsp;
as well as control mice to the treatment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:36.120
VO: T cells seemed to be playing a critical role in&nbsp;
fighting off cancer and Lionel was interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:42.960
boosting those abilities, that led him to cytokines&nbsp;
signaling proteins that help control the immune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.262
system; including binding to T cells and making&nbsp;
them more aggressive. 

00:01:47.262 --> 00:02:00.540
LA: We found so far that there is one subset of T cells that secrete a chemical&nbsp;mediator like called interleukin 9 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:08.280
those T cells are particularly potent in&nbsp;
eliminating cancer cells. 

00:02:08.572 --> 00:02:17.760
VO: These cells known&nbsp;as TH9 cells deal with cancer 
by rallying over&nbsp;immune cells to their cause. They secrete the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:24.480
cytokine interleukin 9 which activates other immune&nbsp;
cells to bind to and destroy cancerous tumors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.480
TH9 cells appear to be particularly&nbsp;
effective at dealing with some tumors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.940
in part because they keep their anti-cancer&nbsp;
properties longer than other kinds of cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.600
So, Lionel has been trying to further improve&nbsp;
their abilities.

00:02:40.975 --> 00:02:50.280
LA: So, we are trying to understand&nbsp;how these cells develop, how we 
can further enhance&nbsp;their anti-cancer properties so that then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.822
are really potent in eliminating cancer cells.&nbsp;

00:02:55.822 --> 00:02:59.075
VO: Lionel and his team have found that cytokines are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:05.820
not only produced by the TH9 cells but the cells&nbsp;
are also affected by different kinds of cytokines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.340
In fact, specific mixtures of cytokines&nbsp;
seem to improve their anti-cancer ability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:18.360
Lionel hopes to take samples of TH9 cells from a&nbsp;
person with cancer and convert them into better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:24.120
longer lasting cancer fighters using cytokine&nbsp;
cocktails and then transfer them back into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:30.185
body to fight the tumor. But at the same time&nbsp;
it's a balance they can't be too aggressive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.185 --> 00:03:39.060
LA: And clearly that's a challenge because those cells&nbsp;
might be somehow triggering like unwanted effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:46.980
or they might become harmful after transfer&nbsp;
to individuals. So, that's where it's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:53.520
a balance between like also aggressiveness&nbsp;
against cancer cells, not being harmful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.082
the cells of our own body. 

00:03:55.082 --> 00:03:59.503
VO: But it isn't just&nbsp;aggressiveness that Lionel needs to manage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.628 --> 00:04:07.560
LA: But then, afterwards, after transfer to the patients like&nbsp;
what is important is that those T cells should be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:14.933
keep those properties to mediate their anti-cancer&nbsp;
effect in the long run they should be able to&nbsp;persist. 

00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:23.400
I would be really happy to see, like in&nbsp;
terms of hope, like if we could transfer this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.940
in the clinical setting. Our goal&nbsp;
would be to bring that to a phase one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:37.140
first clinical trial and then, ideally, test&nbsp;
if those TH9 cells can be successful in treating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:45.360
patients like in possibly phase two clinical trials.&nbsp;
I know the stakes and it's hard but sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:54.060
the hardest part was really like when I when&nbsp;
I meet with patients, because then, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:03.120
in a way it feels rewarding, because like we are&nbsp;
trying really to help them, but it's also very, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.717
on the emotional point of view, that's also a major challenge.

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:22.284
Because it shows also that we need to pursue, we&nbsp;need to do better. 
 So, it's mixed feelings&nbsp;basically.

00:05:22.284 --> 00:05:30.300
And if, like, we would be able, like, to&nbsp;
again come up with solutions for even like a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:37.740
portion of patients like I would really regard&nbsp;
this like personally as a major achievement.

